{
  // Name matches the Workers Builds dashboard connection (the deployed Worker + its
  // workers.dev URL); a mismatch makes every CI deploy override the name with a warning.
  "name": "magna-carta",
  "main": "./src/index.ts",
  "compatibility_date": "2026-06-05",
  // Better Auth resolves its workerd build to the AsyncLocalStorage-backed transaction context
  // (node:async_hooks) — without Node.js compatibility every auth route 500s at runtime.
  "compatibility_flags": ["nodejs_compat"],
  // Explicit so deploys stop defaulting them: workers.dev serving stays on; per-version preview URLs
  // stay off (previews are disabled until a staging environment exists — ADR-0075).
  //
  // workers.dev is NOT the whole of where this Worker answers, and this comment used to claim it was
  // ("the current public URL until magnacarta.esq attaches") on no evidence but the absence of a
  // `routes` block below. A CUSTOM DOMAIN IS ACCOUNT STATE: attached in the dashboard, present in no
  // `wrangler.jsonc`, and not removed by `wrangler deploy` — so a Worker with `workers_dev: true`
  // and no declared routes can be serving a real apex hostname at the same time. "No routes here,
  // therefore workers.dev only" is the inference to refuse; this file's silence is evidence about
  // this file. Read `GET /accounts/{account_id}/workers/domains/records` for which hostname maps to
  // which Worker, and leave the answer in the terminal rather than writing it back here — that read
  // stays correct after the next dashboard change and a pasted value does not.
  // Runbook: docs/conventions/operations.md.
  "workers_dev": true,
  "preview_urls": false,
  // The wrangler-side half of the dual `.sql`-as-text bundling contract — full story in
  // src/sql-modules.d.ts (the Vite-side half is the sql-as-text plugin in vitest.config.ts).
  "rules": [{ "type": "Text", "globs": ["**/*.sql"], "fallthrough": true }],
  // The SvelteKit SPA (apps/web build output) bound as Workers Static Assets.
  //
  // THE ROUTING RULE, and it runs asset-server-first: Cloudflare serves a matching static asset for
  // every path `run_worker_first` does NOT name, and the Worker is invoked only on a miss. So the
  // array is not a list of API prefixes — it is the list of EVERY top-level path family the Hono app
  // answers on, and a family missing from it is not merely slow to reach: no asset matches it, the
  // not_found_handling below then answers `/index.html` with a 200, and the handler is never entered.
  // The caller gets the app shell with a success status where it expected a document, a byte range or
  // a page, which is why this is listed exhaustively rather than trusted to review. The families:
  //   /api/*            — the whole API surface (the /api/v1 chokepoint routes, Better Auth's
  //                       /api/auth/*, /api/openapi.json, the cookieless files-preview serve path,
  //                       and the dev-only /api/__dev helpers).
  //   /wopi/*           — the editor's data plane (apps/api-worker/src/editing/wopi-host.ts): cookieless
  //                       and access-token-guarded, deliberately outside the chokepoint.
  //   /search-objects/* — the keyword engine's ranged-read surface
  //                       (apps/api-worker/src/search/split-object-host.ts), cookieless for the same
  //                       reason: the caller is a Container holding no session.
  //   /__dev/*          — the throwaway local demo page (apps/api-worker/src/http/routes/developer/dev-web-tools-demo.ts),
  //                       which answers 404 unless DEV_DEMO_TOOLS is set.
  //   /internal/*       — the socket the media routing service dials to hand a meeting recorder a
  //                       track's audio (apps/api-worker/src/routes/meeting-sink.ts): cookieless,
  //                       signed-token-guarded, and refusing any request that carries an Origin.
  // It stays an explicit list rather than inverting to `true` with exceptions so the SPA's own files
  // keep being served by the asset server — free, and off this Worker's CPU and invocation count.
  // apps/api-worker/test/worker-first-routing-scan.test.ts reads this array against the app's live Hono
  // dispatch table and fails on the day a family is added without a line here.
  //
  // SINCE THE 2026-08-31 CUTOVER this block is the LOCAL-DEV VEHICLE AND THE ROLLBACK FALLBACK, not
  // how the deployed site serves its interface. The app's address is split by route pattern on the
  // zone: magna-carta-web (the assets-only sibling, same ../web/build output) serves every path, and
  // this Worker is routed only the five families above — so the zone's route list and
  // run_worker_first must name the SAME families, and a new family means a new zone route (an
  // operator step, docs/conventions/operations.md) in the same breath as the line here. The block
  // stays because `wrangler dev` and the Playwright e2e suite serve the whole app from it on :8787,
  // and because repointing the zone's /* route back to this Worker is the one-gesture rollback —
  // both of those need a replacement before anyone removes it. What actually decouples interface
  // deploys from API deploys is the Build Watch Paths on the magna-carta project (no apps/web/*),
  // which is dashboard state: read it from the API, never from here.
  "assets": {
    "directory": "../web/build",
    "binding": "ASSETS",
    "not_found_handling": "single-page-application",
    "run_worker_first": ["/api/*", "/wopi/*", "/search-objects/*", "/__dev/*", "/internal/*"],
  },
  // Public, non-secret config. FILES_ORIGIN is the cookieless preview/download host; bound here (not
  // .dev.vars) because it is a hostname, not a credential. In production this becomes
  // files.magnacartausercontent.com — the SAME dedicated content domain the editor uses (a separate
  // registrable domain from the app, so served bytes never carry an app cookie); the app worker takes a
  // `files.magnacartausercontent.com/*` zone route, more specific than the editor's wildcard, which is
  // dashboard state like the app's own path families (docs/conventions/operations.md). The placeholder
  // value lets the Miniflare test pool exercise the host branch. The app pages allowed to read the
  // bytes are found from APP_BASE_URL below, not from this host's parent, because the two hosts sit on
  // different domains. The editor half is USERCONTENT_DOMAIN
  // (the bare content domain), which defaults to magnacartausercontent.com in code — no deploy var, prod
  // can't forget it; only local dev overrides it.
  // R2_BUCKET_NAME names the bucket bound below as R2. An R2Bucket handle carries no name, so this is
  // how the bucket resolver (src/files/firm-bucket.ts) proves that the bucket a presigned URL addresses
  // is the same one this Worker reads through. Keep it byte-identical to the r2_buckets bucket_name.
  "vars": {
    "FILES_ORIGIN": "files.example.com",
    "R2_BUCKET_NAME": "magna-carta-production-platform-files",
    // What the two anonymous writes (registration, invitation redemption) do while NO Turnstile
    // secret is provisioned: "fail-open" serves them unprotected and counts every unprotected pass
    // as human_verification.decided/unenforced; "fail-closed" refuses with a 503. Stated here rather
    // than left to the code default so changing the posture is this one line. It has no effect once
    // TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY is set — from then on a missing or bad solution is refused either way.
    "HUMAN_VERIFICATION_POSTURE": "fail-open",
    // The switch that selects the Cloudflare Email Service adapter over the logging one
    // (src/auth-instance.ts emailAdapterForEnv). magnacarta.esq is onboarded for Email Sending, and the send
    // paths are guarded now: a refusal answers a retryable 503 carrying an Error ID rather than
    // failing its route, and the sends Better Auth owns are caught at the port and counted. While
    // Email Sending is in beta a destination may still need verifying in the account before it will
    // accept a send — that shows up as email.delivery_failed, not as a broken route.
    "EMAIL_FROM": "no-reply@magnacarta.esq",
    // The origin for the passes that have NO request to derive one from, and now also what Better
    // Auth resolves its baseURL against — without it the library warns that callbacks and redirects
    // may not work, because it is left inferring the origin per request.
    "APP_BASE_URL": "https://magnacarta.esq",
  },
  // Real production resources (created 2026-06-09; naming convention in ADR-0075:
  // magna-carta-<environment>-<scope>[-<purpose-or-slug>]). Binding names are the code-facing
  // contract and never change; resource names are environment-scoped and permanent (D1/R2
  // cannot be renamed). Local dev/tests run Miniflare simulations of these bindings.
  "d1_databases": [
    {
      "binding": "DB_CONTROL",
      "database_name": "magna-carta-production-platform-control",
      "database_id": "a0982ab7-97d9-471b-8dd7-19f18aa53f04",
    },
    // Per-firm data lives in the firmId-addressed FirmDatabase Durable Object (the FIRM_DATABASE
    // namespace below), which self-migrates on first access — there is no per-firm D1 pool to
    // pre-declare here.
  ],
  // Runtime secrets this Worker requires — set in the Cloudflare dashboard (Worker → Settings →
  // Variables and Secrets) or with `wrangler secret put`. Declaring them here makes `wrangler deploy`
  // fail loudly with a missing-secret list rather than letting auth routes 500 by design at runtime.
  // Locally, wrangler only WARNS about a missing name — which is what lets the two Cloudflare
  // credentials below stay out of .dev.vars, where their presence would select the real adapters.
  //
  // CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN_FIRM_PROVISIONER / CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID select the REST FirmResourceProvider
  // and the SigV4 presigner; absent, the development adapters run — which record a bucket name for
  // every new firm and create no bucket, and route uploads through this Worker. That is right on a
  // developer's machine and silently wrong in production: a deployment ran that way and every firm
  // it provisioned could not upload, with nothing reporting why. So they are required here, and a
  // deploy without them stops at the gate instead of provisioning firms it cannot serve.
  // R2_S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID / R2_S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY: the R2 S3 API token the upload presigner signs
  // direct-to-R2 URLs with, "Apply to all buckets in this account" — every firm has its own bucket,
  // and a token scoped to one cannot sign for any other.
  //
  // OPTIONAL, AND DELIBERATELY NOT REQUIRED: MEETING_SINK_SIGNING_KEY, the secret a meeting recorder
  // signs each track's sink address with (src/meetings/sink-token.ts). Absent is the shipped state:
  // recording answers 503 meeting-recording-not-configured and offers no Record control, exactly as
  // REALTIME_SFU_APP_ID / REALTIME_SFU_APP_SECRET absent means no meetings at all. Listing it here
  // would fail every deploy until the owner has proved the routing service keeps the sink address
  // intact and set it with `wrangler secret put MEETING_SINK_SIGNING_KEY`.
  "secrets": {
    "required": [
      "AUTH_SECRET",
      "CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID",
      "CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN_FIRM_PROVISIONER",
      "PLATFORM_ADMIN_KEY",
      "R2_S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID",
      "R2_S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY",
      "ROOT_KEK",
    ],
  },
  "r2_buckets": [{ "binding": "R2", "bucket_name": "magna-carta-production-platform-files" }],
  // The platform-status KV namespace: holds the operator-authored document (status:authored, written by
  // the platform-status routes) and the cron-derived document (status:derived); the read path merges
  // both. This id MUST stay identical to the STATUS_KV id in apps/status-worker/wrangler.jsonc; both
  // bind the one real "Magna_Carta_Status" namespace.
  "kv_namespaces": [{ "binding": "STATUS_KV", "id": "6746720bd1ad4638ad00370197f49788" }],
  // Workers AI — the authenticated binding the inference foundation reaches the AI Gateway through.
  // No per-firm provider key at launch (Unified Billing, Cloudflare-managed credentials).
  "ai": { "binding": "AI" },
  // Cloudflare Flagship feature-flag binding omitted here: Miniflare (the local test runtime)
  // rejects the "flagship" key because it expects an array, not an object. The binding is a
  // production-only wiring step — add it in the deploy runbook once the Flagship provider is
  // provisioned. The optional FLAGS? type on Bindings and resolveFlagEvaluator already handle its
  // absence everywhere: the resolver falls back to defaultOffEvaluator (stable behavior).
  // Production wrangler.jsonc snippet (add manually at deploy time):
  //   "flagship": { "binding": "FLAGS" },
  // FILES_INGEST carries one R2 event per uploaded object to the ingest consumer (single read:
  // magic-byte gates + streaming SHA-256 + scan port, then commit or quarantine). It is a distinct
  // queue from the platform job queue so upload bursts cannot starve the outbox relay, and so its
  // consumer can be tuned and dead-lettered on its own. The matching `consumers` entry is added with
  // the queue() handler — a declared consumer with no exported handler fails the deploy.
  "queues": {
    "producers": [
      { "binding": "QUEUE", "queue": "magna-carta-production-platform-jobs" },
      { "binding": "FILES_INGEST", "queue": "magna-carta-production-files-ingest" },
      { "binding": "FILES_INGEST_BULK", "queue": "magna-carta-production-files-ingest-bulk" },
      // The async bulk-download build queue: the download route enqueues one build per
      // content-addressed selection; the BundleBuildCoordinator DO is the single-flight producer.
      { "binding": "BUNDLE_BUILD", "queue": "magna-carta-production-bundle-build" },
      { "binding": "INFERENCE", "queue": "magna-carta-production-inference" },
      // The server-conversion queue: the ConversionJob DO enqueues one message per job; the
      // consumer runs the ConversionPort adapter to convert the source to the target format.
      { "binding": "CONVERSION", "queue": "magna-carta-production-conversion" },
      // The recovery shard queue: the RecoveryJobCoordinator DO enqueues one message per shard; the
      // consumer runs a dictionary/rule search over its shard and reports the outcome back.
      { "binding": "RECOVERY_QUEUE", "queue": "magna-carta-production-recovery-shard" },
      // The email-capture queue: the forward/CC Email Worker and the file-to-matter API both enqueue a
      // pointer to the staged raw MIME; the consumer runs parse/dedup/thread-link/attachment off the
      // request path.
      { "binding": "EMAILS_CAPTURE", "queue": "magna-carta-production-emails-capture" },
      // The discovery search-index queue: the SearchIndexJob DO enqueues one message per committed
      // document version; the consumer extracts its text, builds its split, and converges the per-firm
      // split catalog.
      { "binding": "SEARCH_INDEX", "queue": "magna-carta-production-search-index" },
      // The split-compaction queue: the SearchCompactionJob DO enqueues one message per corpus that has
      // crossed the compaction threshold; the consumer merges that corpus's small splits into one and
      // swaps the catalog. What keeps the read path's cost tracking a threshold the platform chooses
      // rather than the number of documents ever indexed.
      { "binding": "SEARCH_COMPACTION", "queue": "magna-carta-production-search-compaction" },
      // The discovery processing lane: the load coordinator's dispatch walk enqueues one review
      // document per message; the consumer reads its bytes through the composite extraction port and
      // ends it in exactly one terminal state. Its own queue, apart from every ingest lane, because a
      // load at the ceiling is a quarter of a million documents and the alternative is a firm's
      // interactive uploads queued behind an opposing party's production.
      { "binding": "DISCOVERY_PROCESS", "queue": "magna-carta-production-discovery-process" },
      // The document-assembly queue: the DocumentAssemblyJob DO enqueues one message per assembly; the
      // consumer resolves the merge context, merges the template, and files the output through the same
      // upload/scan chokepoint every other byte crosses.
      { "binding": "DOCUMENT_ASSEMBLY", "queue": "magna-carta-production-document-assembly" },
      // The conversational assistant's queue: the per-firm outbox relay hands a submitted turn here
      // (the outbox row rides the same atomic batch as the turn rows it names); the consumer re-reads
      // the firm's consent gates, builds the bounded window, calls the one inference chokepoint, and
      // settles the turn. Model work never runs on the request path.
      { "binding": "MAGNUS", "queue": "magna-carta-production-magnus" },
      // NOT YET BOUND: MEETING_RECORDING_COMPOSE, the meeting-recording queue. The recorder enqueues one
      // message when a stopped recording has every track staged, the consumer re-enqueues it for each
      // next window of the mix, and the transcript and summary ride the same queue as two more kinds
      // of message. A deploy that binds a queue the account does not have fails, and a merge to main
      // deploys, so the producer and its consumer below are added in the change that follows the
      // queue's creation (docs/conventions/operations.md, "Turning meeting recording on"). Until then
      // the binding is absent, the recorder stages nothing it cannot hand on, and no Record control is
      // offered.
    ],
    // The files-ingest consumer (the queue() handler in src/index.ts over the ingest consumer): one
    // R2 object-create event per uploaded object → single-read gates + SHA-256 + scan port → commit
    // or quarantine. A declared consumer with no exported handler fails the deploy, so this lands
    // with the handler. Dead-letters to its own queue after max retries (the 0·2 relay discipline).
    // Both the interactive files-ingest queue and the bulk files-ingest-bulk queue route to the same
    // ingest handler; the lane separation is entirely in queue topology and max_concurrency.
    "consumers": [
      // The shared per-firm job queue: everything the per-firm outbox relay sweeps crosses this one
      // queue, firmId-tagged, and the dispatch table in src/background/costs/recoverable-charge-consumer.ts
      // routes each message to the handler that owns its topic (unowned topics are acked and
      // ignored). Declaring it a consumer is what makes a per-firm outbox row actually reach its
      // effect — email capture-address minting depends on it to write the control-plane routing row,
      // without which a minted address never receives mail.
      {
        "queue": "magna-carta-production-platform-jobs",
        "dead_letter_queue": "magna-carta-production-platform-jobs-deadletter",
      },
      {
        // The interactive lane: a member is watching. One second of batch wait rather than the
        // five-second default (a single upload otherwise sits in the queue waiting for company),
        // and ten retries with the consumer's own backoff, so a scan engine that is cold-starting
        // or being redeployed (a minute or two of 500s) can never dead-letter a file the member
        // just sent — dead-lettered files wait for the five-minute redrive, which is the "it works
        // if I wait long enough" a member reported.
        "queue": "magna-carta-production-files-ingest",
        "max_batch_timeout": 1,
        "max_retries": 10,
        "dead_letter_queue": "magna-carta-production-files-ingest-deadletter",
      },
      {
        // The bulk lane (migration / backfill). Higher concurrency than the interactive lane because
        // this is where throughput is won: many files scanned in parallel across the autoscaling scan
        // Container fleet. The ceiling here should track the deployed container `max_instances` × the
        // per-instance concurrency the instance memory allows — and the SCAN+mmap transport (Option B,
        // ~1× resident vs INSTREAM's ~2×) is what lets each instance run several large scans at once,
        // so this lane can stay saturated. Raise both together for a large migration burst (the
        // tuning lives in the Malware-scan Container deploy section of docs/conventions/operations.md);
        // 10 is a sane steady-state default that a
        // small fleet absorbs without overload.
        "queue": "magna-carta-production-files-ingest-bulk",
        "max_concurrency": 10,
        "max_retries": 10,
        "dead_letter_queue": "magna-carta-production-files-ingest-bulk-deadletter",
      },
      // The bundle-build consumer (the queue() handler routes this queue to the bundle consumer):
      // one content-addressed selection per message → R2 bodies streamed through client-zip into
      // bundles/<manifestHash>.zip. Low concurrency (a build is bandwidth-bound); dead-letters to its
      // own queue after max retries so a wedged build never blocks the lane.
      {
        "queue": "magna-carta-production-bundle-build",
        "max_concurrency": 2,
        "dead_letter_queue": "magna-carta-production-bundle-build-deadletter",
      },
      {
        "queue": "magna-carta-production-inference",
        "dead_letter_queue": "magna-carta-production-inference-deadletter",
      },
      // The conversion consumer (the queue() handler routes this queue to the conversion consumer):
      // one ConversionJob DO message per job → ConversionPort adapter → R2 result. Low concurrency
      // (conversion is CPU/memory-bound in the engine Container); dead-letters to its own queue
      // after max retries so a wedged conversion never blocks the lane.
      {
        "queue": "magna-carta-production-conversion",
        "max_concurrency": 2,
        "dead_letter_queue": "magna-carta-production-conversion-deadletter",
      },
      // The recovery shard consumer (the queue() handler routes this queue to the recovery
      // consumer): one dictionary/rule shard per message, reporting its outcome back to the
      // RecoveryJobCoordinator DO. Concurrency tracks the shard fan-out width.
      {
        "queue": "magna-carta-production-recovery-shard",
        "max_concurrency": 4,
        "dead_letter_queue": "magna-carta-production-recovery-shard-deadletter",
      },
      // The email-capture consumer (the queue() handler routes this queue to the capture consumer):
      // one filing job per message → parse, dedup, thread-link, stage the matter link, extract
      // attachments onto the ingest chokepoint. Dead-letters to its own queue after max retries so a
      // poison message never blocks the lane.
      {
        "queue": "magna-carta-production-emails-capture",
        "dead_letter_queue": "magna-carta-production-emails-capture-deadletter",
      },
      // The search-index consumer (the queue() handler routes this queue to the index consumer): one
      // committed document version per message → extract text → build a split through the
      // SearchIndexPort → replace the document's catalog rows. Low concurrency: each message drives a
      // recognition pass and an index build, both CPU-bound in their engine Containers. The dead-letter
      // queue is load-bearing rather than hygiene — a message that lands there IS the record that a
      // document is missing from the searchable corpus, which discovery has to be able to answer for.
      {
        "queue": "magna-carta-production-search-index",
        "max_concurrency": 2,
        "dead_letter_queue": "magna-carta-production-search-index-deadletter",
      },
      // The split-compaction consumer: one corpus per message → merge its small splits into one through
      // the SearchIndexPort → swap the per-firm catalog in a single batch. Concurrency 1, and the
      // constraint is not CPU: a merge reads and rewrites every byte of its sources, so several running
      // at once would multiply the R2 traffic of background housekeeping against the query traffic it
      // exists to reduce. Per-corpus single-flighting is enforced by the coordinator Durable Object
      // rather than by this lane, which only bounds how much merging the whole worker does at a time.
      {
        "queue": "magna-carta-production-search-compaction",
        "max_concurrency": 1,
        "dead_letter_queue": "magna-carta-production-search-compaction-deadletter",
      },
      // The discovery processing consumer (the queue() handler routes this queue to the processing
      // consumer): one review document per message -> read the source object -> the composite
      // extraction port -> text and sidecar to R2 -> the row's terminal state and the load's counters.
      // Concurrency 4 rather than the index lane's 2: extraction runs in the discovery Container,
      // which is sized for exactly this burst and scales to zero between loads, and the ceiling that
      // matters is the Container's own max_instances rather than this one. Deliberately well below
      // the platform's 250 — a bulk load must not be able to saturate the account's consumer budget
      // and slow the interactive lanes it was separated from.
      //
      // The dead-letter queue is LOAD-BEARING rather than hygiene: a message that lands there IS the
      // record that a document was never read, and the load's reconciliation refuses to complete
      // while its counts do not add up, so a dead letter surfaces as a load that will not close
      // rather than as a document quietly missing from the corpus.
      {
        "queue": "magna-carta-production-discovery-process",
        "max_concurrency": 4,
        "dead_letter_queue": "magna-carta-production-discovery-process-deadletter",
      },
      // The document-assembly consumer (the queue() handler routes this queue to the assembly
      // consumer): one job per message → resolve the merge context → merge over the already-normalized
      // template → file the output through initiateUpload/completeUploadSession. The work is a
      // decompress, an XML walk, and a recompress over tens to hundreds of kilobytes, so the lane is
      // not concurrency-starved the way a container-backed engine lane is; the dead-letter queue is
      // where a job that repeatedly fails transport lands, and a job that failed for a business reason
      // never reaches it (a typed failure is recorded and acked).
      {
        "queue": "magna-carta-production-document-assembly",
        "max_concurrency": 4,
        "dead_letter_queue": "magna-carta-production-document-assembly-deadletter",
      },
      // The conversational assistant's consumer (the queue() handler routes this queue to the
      // assistant consumer): one pending turn per message → re-read the gates → build the bounded
      // window → one call through the inference chokepoint → settle the turn, meter it, and push the
      // transition. Its own lane rather than the shared per-firm bus so a slow generation cannot
      // delay an unrelated per-firm effect, and its own dead-letter queue so a message that keeps
      // failing transport is visible instead of silently retried forever. A turn that failed for a
      // business reason never reaches the dead-letter queue: it is settled with a closed label and
      // acked, because retrying cannot fix a closed consent gate.
      {
        "queue": "magna-carta-production-magnus",
        "max_concurrency": 4,
        "dead_letter_queue": "magna-carta-production-magnus-deadletter",
      },
    ],
  },
  // Worker service bindings to dedicated sibling workers. JSONC keeps only the LAST duplicate top-level
  // key, so ALL bindings must live in this one array. Absent in tests unless stubs resolve them (see
  // vitest.config.ts); each optional binding has a graceful fallback in production code.
  //   SCAN_SERVICE: the malware-scan worker (resolveScanPort falls back to the no-engine adapter).
  //   BUNDLE_SERVICE: the bundle-build worker (POST /build); the bundle lanes 503 until it is bound.
  //   CONVERT: the conversion engine worker (resolveEnginePort returns the deferred port while unbound).
  //   FILE_REPAIR_SERVICE: the file-repair engine worker (POST /diagnose|/repair|/carve); the file-repair
  //     routes 503 until it is bound.
  //   RECOVERY_SERVICE: the password-recovery worker; optional, falls back when absent.
  //   OCR_SERVICE: the text-recognition engine worker (POST /ocr); the ocr route 503s until it is bound.
  //   SIGN_SERVICE: the e-signature/seal worker (POST /seal), which alone holds the platform signing
  //     key; the documents/seal route 503s until it is bound (deferred activation).
  //   DISCOVERY_SERVICE: the pure-Rust discovery engine worker (POST /read|/identify|/digest|
  //     /archive/*). While unbound the composite extraction port falls back to the in-Worker PDF
  //     reader plus recognition, which reads strictly less — every non-PDF review document parks
  //     rather than being recorded unreadable.
  //   EDIT_SERVICE / STATUS_SERVICE / WEB_SERVICE: version-read-only bindings (see the note on
  //     them below); nothing but GET /api/v1/meta/versions calls through them.
  "services": [
    { "binding": "SCAN_SERVICE", "service": "magna-carta-scan" },
    { "binding": "BUNDLE_SERVICE", "service": "magna-carta-bundle" },
    { "binding": "CONVERT", "service": "magna-carta-convert" },
    { "binding": "FILE_REPAIR_SERVICE", "service": "magna-carta-file-repair" },
    { "binding": "RECOVERY_SERVICE", "service": "magna-carta-recovery" },
    { "binding": "OCR_SERVICE", "service": "magna-carta-ocr" },
    { "binding": "SIGN_SERVICE", "service": "magna-carta-sign" },
    { "binding": "DISCOVERY_SERVICE", "service": "magna-carta-discovery" },
    // The keyword search engine (apps/search-worker). This one line is what turns searching inside
    // files on for the deployment: with it, resolveSearchIndexPort (src/search/search-engine.ts)
    // returns the port over this binding in place of the deferred one, the index queue's parked
    // documents are attempted again (the engine generation in src/search/engine-generation.ts
    // changes), and a matter's search, the conflicts check and a discovery review set can ask the
    // engine. A firm still reads nothing inside its files until its own switch is on (Settings,
    // Search and Indexing). The worker is on the account: the owner confirmed it on 2026-11-13.
    // Like every entry here, it must name a worker that exists or THIS worker's deploy fails
    // (code 10143).
    { "binding": "SEARCH_SERVICE", "service": "magna-carta-search" },
    // The three below exist ONLY for the aggregate version read (GET /api/v1/meta/versions): the
    // edit worker's real traffic flows the other way (its APP_WORKER binding), the status page
    // reads KV, and the web worker is assets-only. A service binding must name a worker that
    // exists or THIS worker's deploy fails (code 10143) — all three are on the account
    // (magna-carta-edit connected and green, magna-carta-status holding the status hostname,
    // magna-carta-web connected on main). EMAIL_SERVICE is deliberately NOT here for the same
    // rule: magna-carta-email is not on the account yet — add
    // { "binding": "EMAIL_SERVICE", "service": "magna-carta-email" } with that worker's first
    // deploy (docs/deploy-queue.md, email capture activation), and its version row reads
    // "Not deployed" until then.
    { "binding": "EDIT_SERVICE", "service": "magna-carta-edit" },
    { "binding": "STATUS_SERVICE", "service": "magna-carta-status" },
    { "binding": "WEB_SERVICE", "service": "magna-carta-web" },
  ],
  // MANAGED NATURAL-LANGUAGE SEARCH — the two bindings, both DECLARED AND COMMENTED OUT.
  //
  // They are written here rather than left to be invented on the day they are needed, because the
  // shapes are load-bearing and the resolution path they feed is already built and already runs:
  // apps/api-worker/src/search/natural-language-binding.ts selects on the presence of each, and an
  // absent binding resolves to a port whose every operation reports the feature unavailable. So a
  // deployment that uncomments these gains a working feature and one that does not is honestly
  // broken rather than quietly empty.
  //
  // They stay commented out because UNCOMMENTING THEM IS A DECISION, not a configuration step.
  // `ai_search_namespaces` is the management binding, and the first instance it creates fixes an
  // embedding model that cannot be changed afterwards without re-embedding the corpus — the
  // platform's embedding pin (packages/inference/src/catalog.ts) must be frozen before that happens.
  // `ai_search` is the query binding, and the retrieval adapter behind it is deliberately unwritten
  // until the managed index's metadata-filter semantics have been verified against the live service.
  //
  // "ai_search_namespaces": [{ "binding": "AI_SEARCH_NAMESPACES" }],
  // "ai_search": [{ "binding": "AI_SEARCH", "instance": "<the derived per-firm instance name>" }],
  // Browser Rendering Workers binding — used by POST /api/v1/safe-browse to render suspect URLs
  // in Cloudflare's isolated remote browser. Requires compatibility_date >= 2026-03-24 for
  // quickAction(); the current compatibility_date (2026-06-05) already satisfies that requirement.
  "browser": { "binding": "BROWSER" },
  // The async bulk-download coordinator — the codebase's first Durable Object. Keyed
  // firmId:manifestHash, it single-flights one ZIP build per content-addressed file selection. The
  // class is exported from src/index.ts (a binding to a class with no export fails the deploy).
  // EVERY Durable Object binding MUST live in this one block: JSONC keeps only the last duplicate
  // top-level key, so a second "durable_objects" (or "migrations") key silently drops every binding
  // declared above it. DOCUMENT_SESSION (slice 2·2) coordinates one per-document editing session
  // (participants + current working-object metadata + the WOPI session lock), SQLite-backed so its
  // state survives the editing container sleeping. FIRM_DATABASE is the per-firm SQLite-backed DO (one
  // instance per firm, idFromName(firmId), self-migrating on wake, the data-plane RPC target for all
  // per-firm SQL). DURABLE_TIMER is the per-timekeeper live-timer coordinator — one instance per
  // `${firmId}:${userId}` (firm-namespaced, so a timer never holds another firm's state), holding only
  // transient live-timer state; a committed time entry flows through the data-access chokepoint into
  // the per-firm database, never the DO.
  "durable_objects": {
    "bindings": [
      { "name": "BUNDLE_COORDINATOR", "class_name": "BundleBuildCoordinator" },
      { "name": "DOCUMENT_SESSION", "class_name": "DocumentSession" },
      // The conversion-job coordinator: keyed firmId:jobId, single-flights one conversion per job.
      { "name": "CONVERSION_JOB", "class_name": "ConversionJob" },
      { "name": "DURABLE_TIMER", "class_name": "LiveTimerDurableObject" },
      // The per-firm SQLite-backed database: one instance per firm (idFromName(firmId)), self-migrating
      // on wake, and the data-plane RPC target for all per-firm SQL.
      { "name": "FIRM_DATABASE", "class_name": "FirmDatabase" },
      // One live browsing session per member (idFromName(`${firmId}:${userId}`), firm-namespaced so
      // a session cannot be addressed across firms). Holds the remote browser's session id and an
      // idle alarm, never the browser handle and never any browsing history.
      { "name": "SAFE_BROWSE_SESSION", "class_name": "SafeBrowseSession" },
      // The password-recovery job coordinator: fans a recovery job out across shards and aggregates
      // outcomes into the job's final state.
      { "name": "RECOVERY_JOB", "class_name": "RecoveryJobCoordinator" },
      // The search-index job coordinator: keyed firmId:documentId:version, single-flights one split
      // build per document version and holds its outcome, including the parked `deferred` state.
      { "name": "SEARCH_INDEX_JOB", "class_name": "SearchIndexJob" },
      // The split-compaction coordinator: keyed firmId:corpusId. Keyed by the CORPUS because two
      // concurrent passes over one corpus would each merge overlapping splits, leaving every document in
      // the overlap live in two merged splits at once.
      { "name": "SEARCH_COMPACTION_JOB", "class_name": "SearchCompactionJob" },
      // The document-assembly job coordinator: keyed firmId:assemblyId, single-flights one assembly per
      // job so a retried submit can never file two documents onto a matter.
      { "name": "DOCUMENT_ASSEMBLY_JOB", "class_name": "DocumentAssemblyJob" },
      // The conversational assistant's per-conversation push object: keyed firmId:conversationId. It
      // stores NOTHING — it reads a turn's state back from the firm's own database and pushes it, so
      // exactly one copy of a transcript exists and it is in the firm's database.
      { "name": "MAGNUS_CONVERSATION", "class_name": "MagnusConversation" },
      // One discovery load's fan-out coordinator: keyed firmId:loadId. It holds the load's CURSOR in
      // durable storage and nothing on the instance, so eviction, deploy, and hibernation are all
      // lossless — a load at the ceiling is a quarter of a million documents.
      { "name": "DISCOVERY_LOAD_JOB", "class_name": "DiscoveryLoadCoordinator" },
      // One meeting's signaling room: keyed firmId:meetingId. It brokers each browser's session with
      // the media routing service and keeps the transient roster; the audio, video and screen share
      // themselves are routed between browsers and never reach this Worker, so nothing anybody said
      // is ever in this object's storage.
      { "name": "MEETING_ROOM", "class_name": "MeetingRoom" },
      // One recording's sink: keyed firmId:recordingId. The routing service's WebSocket adapters dial
      // it, one socket per recorded track, and it stages each track's audio in the firm's own bucket.
      // NOT hibernating — it receives fifty frames a second per track while it works — and it writes no
      // audio to its own storage, only each track's upload and counters.
      { "name": "MEETING_RECORDER", "class_name": "MeetingRecorder" },
      // One meeting's live captions: keyed firmId:meetingId. The routing service's WebSocket adapters
      // dial it for whoever is speaking, and it streams their audio to the caption model and hands the
      // words to the room. A sibling of the recorder rather than a part of it: captions belong to the
      // meeting and run with or without a recording, and the recorder's memory is already spent on its
      // eleven tracks. NOT hibernating while a session is open; it stores no audio and no words, only
      // the handles of the adapters it holds open and counts.
      { "name": "MEETING_CAPTIONER", "class_name": "MeetingCaptioner" },
      // One connected calendar account's sync passes (idFromName(`${firmId}:${connectionId}`),
      // firm-namespaced). Each pass writes a member's events to their own calendar at their provider
      // and records what is there; running them one at a time per account is what stops two passes
      // each making a copy of the same event. It holds who it is for and a retry count, never an
      // event or a token.
      { "name": "CONNECTION_SYNC", "class_name": "ConnectionSync" },
    ],
  },
  // SQLite-backed DO classes are introduced through a migration tag — ONE consolidated lineage (a
  // second "migrations" key would override this one). The tag chain is append-only and MUST match the
  // sequence already applied on the deployed worker (Cloudflare rejects a deploy that drops or renames
  // an applied tag), so the names follow the order they actually landed in: v1 the bulk-download +
  // conversion-job coordinators; v1-live-timer the live-timer coordinator; v1-editing-document-session
  // the editing document-session DO; v2-firm-database the per-firm database DO; v3-recovery-job the
  // password-recovery job coordinator DO; v4-search-index-job the discovery search-index job
  // coordinator DO; v5-document-assembly-job the document-assembly job coordinator DO;
  // v6-magnus-conversation the assistant's per-conversation push object; v7-discovery-load-job the
  // discovery load fan-out coordinator; v8-search-compaction-job the split-compaction coordinator;
  // v9-meeting-room one meeting's signaling room; v10-safe-browse-session one member's live browsing
  // session; v11-meeting-recorder one recording's sink; v12-meeting-captioner one meeting's live
  // captions; v13-connection-sync one connected calendar account's sync passes (the most recent
  // class, appended last). Never modify an applied tag — only append a new
  // one.
  "migrations": [
    { "tag": "v1", "new_sqlite_classes": ["BundleBuildCoordinator", "ConversionJob"] },
    { "tag": "v1-live-timer", "new_sqlite_classes": ["LiveTimerDurableObject"] },
    { "tag": "v1-editing-document-session", "new_sqlite_classes": ["DocumentSession"] },
    { "tag": "v2-firm-database", "new_sqlite_classes": ["FirmDatabase"] },
    { "tag": "v3-recovery-job", "new_sqlite_classes": ["RecoveryJobCoordinator"] },
    { "tag": "v4-search-index-job", "new_sqlite_classes": ["SearchIndexJob"] },
    { "tag": "v5-document-assembly-job", "new_sqlite_classes": ["DocumentAssemblyJob"] },
    { "tag": "v6-magnus-conversation", "new_sqlite_classes": ["MagnusConversation"] },
    { "tag": "v7-discovery-load-job", "new_sqlite_classes": ["DiscoveryLoadCoordinator"] },
    { "tag": "v8-search-compaction-job", "new_sqlite_classes": ["SearchCompactionJob"] },
    { "tag": "v9-meeting-room", "new_sqlite_classes": ["MeetingRoom"] },
    { "tag": "v10-safe-browse-session", "new_sqlite_classes": ["SafeBrowseSession"] },
    { "tag": "v11-meeting-recorder", "new_sqlite_classes": ["MeetingRecorder"] },
    { "tag": "v12-meeting-captioner", "new_sqlite_classes": ["MeetingCaptioner"] },
    { "tag": "v13-connection-sync", "new_sqlite_classes": ["ConnectionSync"] },
  ],
  // Workers Logs at full sampling (current volumes; the golden-rule-4 structured-JSON event lines
  // the telemetry Workers logger emits land here). Logpush → R2 is a deploy-time runbook step
  // (docs/conventions/operations.md), not code — it is account configuration (slice 0·6, ADR-0074).
  "observability": { "enabled": true, "head_sampling_rate": 1 },
  // The Analytics Engine dataset the telemetry Workers logger mirrors counters to (slice 0·6). The
  // TELEMETRY binding is optional in code (a missing binding degrades to console-only); declaring it
  // here wires the real dataset in production and a local stub under Miniflare.
  "analytics_engine_datasets": [
    { "binding": "TELEMETRY", "dataset": "magna-carta-production-telemetry" },
  ],
  // Cloudflare Email Service send binding (slice 0·6, ADR-0074). The adapter is SELECTED only when
  // EMAIL_FROM is also set — deliberately NOT committed as a var here: it is the activation switch
  // the deploy-time runbook flips once magnacarta.esq is onboarded for Email Sending (DKIM/SPF —
  // docs/conventions/operations.md). Until then the binding is present but unused and production
  // keeps the logging adapter, so no send fails against an un-onboarded domain.
  "send_email": [{ "name": "SEND_EMAIL" }],
  // The scheduled work (src/background/scheduled.ts): the */5 sweep runs the outbox relay over the control-plane
  // + per-firm lineages, the idempotency TTL purge, and the stale-pending upload sweep; the daily
  // 03:00 UTC pass runs the storage-metering reconciliation (a full R2 LIST per firm — too heavy for
  // every five minutes); the weekly 04:00 UTC Sunday pass re-enqueues committed versions still
  // marked scan_verdict = 'unscanned' onto FILES_INGEST so a now-available scan engine can evaluate
  // them in place; the */10 pass probes the core primitives and polls the upstream provider feeds,
  // writing the status:derived snapshot in STATUS_KV; the hourly pass composes notification email
  // digests, waking every hour only because a summary is anchored to each firm's OWN morning rather
  // than to UTC's — firms whose local hour it is not cost two small setting reads, and a deployment
  // with no outbound-mail provisioning exits before reading a single firm. src/index.ts routes each
  // Cron expression to its handler.
  // THREE, and this set is known-good: it deployed successfully on 2026-08-21, the first deploy in
  // seven weeks. The two dropped from it are `0 * * * *` (the hourly notification digest) and
  // `0 4 * * 0` (the weekly natural-language search keep-alive), added 2026-08-16. NEITHER HAS EVER
  // FIRED IN PRODUCTION, because no deploy carrying them ever succeeded. Dropping a trigger deletes no
  // code: the branch in src/index.ts stays, and restoring the line is the whole of turning it back on.
  //
  // Why they failed, corrected — an earlier note here blamed an account ceiling on the number of
  // triggers, and that was wrong twice over. The write is ONE atomic PUT of the whole set (wrangler
  // resolves `config.triggers?.crons` and PUTs .../schedules once), so a single malformed expression
  // refuses ALL of them; "three work, five do not" looked like a count ceiling and never was. And
  // `0 4 * * 0` is malformed: Cloudflare's cron parser numbers days of the week 1 = Sunday through
  // 7 = Saturday, so 0 is out of range, where almost every other cron implementation reads it as
  // Sunday. A weekly Sunday trigger here is `0 4 * * 1` — the constant in src/index.ts now says so.
  // The error's empty body is a reporting artifact rather than an empty response: wrangler puts the
  // API's errors[] into a field its trigger aggregation does not print, so the absence of a code was
  // never evidence about what Cloudflare returned.
  //
  // That correction is untested against this account: the deploy that went green carried three, not
  // four. Restoring `0 * * * *` alone would settle it — nothing in that expression is malformed, so a
  // refusal would mean a real ceiling and a success would mean the day-of-week rule was the whole of
  // it. Add them back ONE AT A TIME either way, because the atomic PUT hides which one was rejected.
  //
  // Omitting the block entirely is a third, different gesture: with no `triggers` key wrangler skips
  // the schedule call altogether and leaves whatever is attached alone. `"crons": []` is NOT that —
  // an empty array is truthy, so it PUTs an empty set and DELETES every schedule the Worker has.
  //
  // `*/5 * * * *` is the one that must never be traded away. It is the SOLE applier of control-plane
  // migrations (src/background/scheduled.ts runs them as the pass's first action), single-flight by construction
  // because a cron fires once per tick and the migration runner is not concurrency-safe. Nothing else
  // in the system applies them — not the deploy, not any request path.
  "triggers": {
    "crons": ["*/5 * * * *", "0 3 * * *", "*/10 * * * *"],
  },
}
